mechanism:
LG2_APPROX_f64 is defined with a fixed asm string "lg2.approx.f64" and bound to the CUDA-reachable builtin intrinsic int_nvvm_lg2_approx_d (an NVVMBuiltin = __nvvm_lg2_approx_d):

  def LG2_APPROX_f64 :
    BasicNVPTXInst<(outs B64:$dst), (ins B64:$src),
      "lg2.approx.f64",
      [(set f64:$dst, (flog2 f64:$src))]>;
  ...
  def : Pat<(int_nvvm_lg2_approx_d f64:$a), (LG2_APPROX_f64 $a)>;

The PTX ISA documents lg2 with exactly one form. From the PTX ISA 9.0 spec (section 9.7.3.20, extracted verbatim from the official PDF):

  Syntax
  lg2.approx{.ftz}.f32       d, a;

There is no .f64 (double-precision) form of lg2.approx in ANY PTX ISA version (verified across 8.5, 8.7, 9.0: grepping the full spec text for lg2 + f64 returns nothing; the only lg2 instruction is lg2.approx{.ftz}.f32). The sibling transcendentals confirm the pattern: sin.approx{.ftz}.f32, cos.approx{.ftz}.f32, ex2.approx{.ftz}.f32 are all f32-only, and the half-precision ex2 forms (ex2.approx.f16 / ex2.approx.ftz.bf16) are explicitly enumerated; none of these has an f64 form either. By contrast the f64 approximate ops that ARE valid have dedicated ISA sections (rcp.approx.ftz.f64 in 9.7.3.14, rsqrt.approx.ftz.f64 in 9.7.3.17) - lg2 has no such section. Thus emitting a bare "lg2.approx.f64" produces an instruction the assembler does not define. CAVEAT: I could not run ptxas in this environment; because __nvvm_lg2_approx_d is a long-standing CUDA builtin, it is conceivable ptxas accepts lg2.approx.f64 as an undocumented instruction (nvcc would have to emit it too). The finding rests on the instruction's complete absence from the public PTX ISA across all versions checked.

