mechanism:
The CVT_TO_TF32 multiclass builds the asm string as "cvt." # Modifier # ".tf32.f32". For the relu+satfinite intrinsics it is instantiated with the literal Modifier "rn.relu.satfinite" / "rz.relu.satfinite":

  defm CVT_to_tf32_rn_relu_satf : CVT_TO_TF32<"rn.relu.satfinite", [hasPTX<86>, hasSM<100>]>;
  defm CVT_to_tf32_rz_relu_satf : CVT_TO_TF32<"rz.relu.satfinite", [hasPTX<86>, hasSM<100>]>;

This emits `cvt.rn.relu.satfinite.tf32.f32` (and the .rz variant) -- .relu printed BEFORE .satfinite.

PTX ISA (verified identical in releases 8.8, 9.0, 9.3, section 9.7.9.21 'Data Movement and Conversion Instructions: cvt') gives the tf32 grammar as:
    cvt.rna{.satfinite}.tf32.f32        d, a;
    cvt.frnd2{.satfinite}{.relu}.tf32.f32   d, a;
i.e. for the tf32 destination the optional .satfinite must precede the optional .relu. This is a DELIBERATE asymmetry in the ISA: the f16/bf16/f16x2/bf16x2 forms are spelled `cvt.frnd2{.relu}{.satfinite}.<t>.f32` (relu first), but tf32 is `{.satfinite}{.relu}` (satfinite first). The LLVM code correctly uses relu-before-satfinite for all the f16/bf16 destinations (CVT_FROM_FLOAT_SATFINITE, CVT_FROM_FLOAT_V2_SM80, etc., all verified against the grammar) but reused that same relu-first order for tf32, which violates the tf32 grammar. PTX modifier order is token-position-fixed in this family (cf. already-found bug #038 where add/sub `.sat.ftz` is rejected by ptxas because the grammar is `.ftz.sat`), so a strict ptxas should reject `cvt.rn.relu.satfinite.tf32.f32` and require `cvt.rn.satfinite.relu.tf32.f32`.

CAVEAT: the commit that introduced these forms (3b5e9eed2f67, PR #124316) states its tests were 'verified with a 12.8 ptxas executable', which raises the possibility that ptxas is lenient about cvt modifier ordering and accepts the emitted form. I could not run ptxas in this environment to settle whether ptxas enforces the grammar's order here. Hence moderate confidence. The plain forms (cvt.rn.tf32.f32, cvt.rn.relu.tf32.f32, cvt.rna.tf32.f32, cvt.rn.satfinite.tf32.f32 with no relu) are all correctly ordered; only the relu+satfinite combination is affected.

